This directory contains multiple ways to export the diffusion-pipe Conda environment.
Each method serves a different purpose depending on how portable or reproducible you want the environment to be.

1. environment_portable.yml — Portable, Recommended

Best for:

Recreating the environment on different machines

Unknown future OS/architectures

Sharing the environment with collaborators

CI systems / cloud servers

What it contains:

All package names and versions

No build numbers or platform-specific details

Why it's good:

Highest compatibility: can usually recreate on Linux, macOS, or different hardware (x86/ARM).

The most reliable export for long-term use.

Command used:

conda env export --name diffusion-pipe --no-builds > environment_portable.yml

2. environment_exact.yml — Full Reproducibility

Best for:

Reproducing exactly the same environment

Same OS, same CPU/GPU architecture

Debugging, experiments, or paper reproducibility

What it contains:

All packages

Exact versions

Build numbers

Channel metadata

Platform-specific info (e.g., linux-64)

Why it's good:

Guarantees the environment is recreated bit-for-bit identical (as long as platform is the same).

Why not always use it:

Not portable — may fail on different OS (Linux → macOS, ARM → x86).

Larger and more rigid.

Command used:

conda env export --name diffusion-pipe > environment_exact.yml

3. packages.txt — Simple Package List

Best for:

Human-readable reference of installed packages

Comparing environments

Quick browsing

What it contains:

Package list in Conda’s --export format

Similar to a requirements.txt but not directly a full environment spec

Why it's good:

Simple, lightweight snapshot

Useful for diffing or debugging

Limitations:

Not enough to rebuild the environment reliably

Missing channel + dependency resolution information

Command used:

conda list --name diffusion-pipe --export > packages.txt